        {
          "topic": "Tree vs General Graph",
          "title": {
            "en": "What makes a graph a tree",
            "es": "Qué hace que un grafo sea un árbol"
          },
          "description": {
            "en": "Tests three graphs against \"connected with n − 1 edges\", shows that adding one edge to a tree closes exactly one cycle, and builds a spanning tree of the campus map with BFS.",
            "es": "Prueba tres grafos con la regla \"conexo con n − 1 aristas\", muestra que agregar una arista a un árbol cierra exactamente un ciclo y construye un árbol generador del mapa del campus con BFS."
          },
          "explanation": {
            "en": "A tree is a connected graph with no cycles. For a graph with n vertices these are equivalent ways to say it is a tree: connected and acyclic; connected with exactly n − 1 edges; exactly one path between every pair of vertices.\n\nWhat to notice in the output:\n- T has 6 vertices, 5 edges and is connected, so it is a tree.\n- Removing C-F leaves 4 edges and cuts F off: a tree minus any edge is disconnected, since every tree edge is a bridge.\n- The campus map is connected but has 6 edges, one too many, so it must contain a cycle (we found it in the Paths and Cycles example).\n- In T there is exactly one path from D to F: D, B, A, C, F. Adding the edge D-F creates a second route, so it closes exactly one cycle: that path plus the new edge.\n- BFS from A keeps only the edge that first discovered each vertex. Those 5 edges form a spanning tree of the campus map: they reach every vertex and leave out C-D, the edge that closed the cycle.\n\nProof idea for n − 1 edges: a tree with n ≥ 2 vertices has a leaf (follow a longest path to its end). Removing the leaf and its single edge leaves a tree on n − 1 vertices, so by induction the edge count is n − 1.",
            "es": "Un árbol es un grafo conexo sin ciclos. Para un grafo con n vértices estas son formas equivalentes de decir que es un árbol: conexo y acíclico; conexo con exactamente n − 1 aristas; exactamente un camino entre cada par de vértices.\n\nQué observar en la salida:\n- T tiene 6 vértices, 5 aristas y es conexo, así que es un árbol.\n- Quitar C-F deja 4 aristas y separa a F: un árbol sin cualquiera de sus aristas queda desconectado, porque cada arista de un árbol es un puente.\n- El mapa del campus es conexo pero tiene 6 aristas, una de más, así que debe tener un ciclo (lo encontramos en el ejemplo de caminos y ciclos).\n- En T hay exactamente un camino de D a F: D, B, A, C, F. Agregar la arista D-F crea una segunda ruta, así que cierra exactamente un ciclo: ese camino más la arista nueva.\n- El BFS desde A conserva solo la arista que descubrió cada vértice. Esas 5 aristas forman un árbol generador del mapa del campus: llegan a todos los vértices y dejan fuera C-D, la arista que cerraba el ciclo.\n\nIdea de la demostración de n − 1 aristas: un árbol con n ≥ 2 vértices tiene una hoja (sigue un camino más largo hasta su final). Quitar la hoja y su única arista deja un árbol con n − 1 vértices, así que por inducción el número de aristas es n − 1."
          },
          "code": {
            "python": "# A tree is connected with exactly n - 1 edges: one path between any two vertices, no cycles\nfrom collections import deque\n\nVERTS = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\"]\nTREE = [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"E\"), (\"C\", \"F\")]\nCAMPUS = [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"E\", \"F\")]\n\n\ndef bfs_parents(verts, edges, start):\n    adj = {v: [] for v in verts}\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n    parent, queue, found = {start: None}, deque([start]), []\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        for w in sorted(adj[u]):\n            if w not in parent:\n                parent[w] = u\n                found.append(f\"{u}-{w}\")  # the edge that discovered w\n                queue.append(w)\n    return parent, found\n\n\ndef check(name, edges):\n    parent, _ = bfs_parents(VERTS, edges, \"A\")\n    connected = len(parent) == len(VERTS)\n    tree = connected and len(edges) == len(VERTS) - 1\n    print(f\"{name}: {len(edges)} edges, connected={'yes' if connected else 'no'} -> {'tree' if tree else 'not a tree'}\")\n\n\ncheck(\"T\", TREE)\ncheck(\"T without C-F\", TREE[:-1])\ncheck(\"Campus map\", CAMPUS)\n\n# Adding any edge to a tree closes exactly one cycle: the tree path between its ends plus the new edge\nparent, _ = bfs_parents(VERTS, TREE, \"D\")\npath, x = [], \"F\"\nwhile x is not None:\n    path.append(x)\n    x = parent[x]\npath.reverse()\nprint(f\"Tree path D to F: {' -> '.join(path)}\")\nprint(f\"Adding D-F closes the cycle: {' -> '.join(path + ['D'])}\")\n\n# BFS keeps only the edge that discovered each vertex: a spanning tree of the campus map\n_, spanning = bfs_parents(VERTS, CAMPUS, \"A\")\nprint(f\"BFS spanning tree of the campus map: {', '.join(spanning)} ({len(spanning)} = {len(VERTS)} - 1 edges)\")\n",
            "java": "import java.util.*;\n\n// A tree is connected with exactly n - 1 edges: one path between any two vertices, no cycles\npublic class TreeVsGraph {\n    static final List<String> VERTS = List.of(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\");\n\n    static List<String[]> edges(String... pairs) {\n        List<String[]> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String p : pairs) out.add(new String[]{p.substring(0, 1), p.substring(1)});\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    // Returns parent pointers; \"found\" collects the edge that discovered each vertex\n    static Map<String, String> bfsParents(List<String[]> edges, String start, List<String> found) {\n        Map<String, List<String>> adj = new HashMap<>();\n        for (String v : VERTS) adj.put(v, new ArrayList<>());\n        for (String[] e : edges) {\n            adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n            adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);\n        }\n        Map<String, String> parent = new HashMap<>();\n        parent.put(start, null);\n        Deque<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(start));\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            String u = queue.poll();\n            List<String> next = new ArrayList<>(adj.get(u));\n            Collections.sort(next);\n            for (String w : next) {\n                if (!parent.containsKey(w)) {\n                    parent.put(w, u);\n                    found.add(u + \"-\" + w);\n                    queue.add(w);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return parent;\n    }\n\n    static void check(String name, List<String[]> edges) {\n        Map<String, String> parent = bfsParents(edges, \"A\", new ArrayList<>());\n        boolean connected = parent.size() == VERTS.size();\n        boolean tree = connected && edges.size() == VERTS.size() - 1;\n        System.out.println(name + \": \" + edges.size() + \" edges, connected=\" + (connected ? \"yes\" : \"no\") + \" -> \" + (tree ? \"tree\" : \"not a tree\"));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String[]> tree = edges(\"AB\", \"AC\", \"BD\", \"BE\", \"CF\");\n        List<String[]> campus = edges(\"AB\", \"AC\", \"BD\", \"CD\", \"DE\", \"EF\");\n        check(\"T\", tree);\n        check(\"T without C-F\", tree.subList(0, tree.size() - 1));\n        check(\"Campus map\", campus);\n\n        // Adding any edge to a tree closes exactly one cycle: the tree path between its ends plus the new edge\n        Map<String, String> parent = bfsParents(tree, \"D\", new ArrayList<>());\n        List<String> path = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String x = \"F\"; x != null; x = parent.get(x)) path.add(x);\n        Collections.reverse(path);\n        System.out.println(\"Tree path D to F: \" + String.join(\" -> \", path));\n        path.add(\"D\");\n        System.out.println(\"Adding D-F closes the cycle: \" + String.join(\" -> \", path));\n\n        // BFS keeps only the edge that discovered each vertex: a spanning tree of the campus map\n        List<String> spanning = new ArrayList<>();\n        bfsParents(campus, \"A\", spanning);\n        System.out.println(\"BFS spanning tree of the campus map: \" + String.join(\", \", spanning) + \" (\" + spanning.size() + \" = \" + VERTS.size() + \" - 1 edges)\");\n    }\n}\n",
            "cpp": "#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <queue>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// A tree is connected with exactly n - 1 edges: one path between any two vertices, no cycles\nusing Edge = pair<string, string>;\nconst vector<string> VERTS = {\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\"};\n\nstring join(const vector<string>& items, const string& sep) {\n    string out;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) out += (i ? sep : \"\") + items[i];\n    return out;\n}\n\n// Returns parent pointers (\"\" for the start); \"found\" collects the edge that discovered each vertex\nmap<string, string> bfsParents(const vector<Edge>& edges, const string& start, vector<string>& found) {\n    map<string, vector<string>> adj;\n    for (auto& v : VERTS) adj[v];\n    for (auto& [u, v] : edges) {\n        adj[u].push_back(v);\n        adj[v].push_back(u);\n    }\n    map<string, string> parent = {{start, \"\"}};\n    queue<string> q;\n    q.push(start);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        string u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        vector<string> next = adj[u];\n        sort(next.begin(), next.end());\n        for (auto& w : next) {\n            if (!parent.count(w)) {\n                parent[w] = u;\n                found.push_back(u + \"-\" + w);\n                q.push(w);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return parent;\n}\n\nvoid check(const string& name, const vector<Edge>& edges) {\n    vector<string> ignored;\n    auto parent = bfsParents(edges, \"A\", ignored);\n    bool connected = parent.size() == VERTS.size();\n    bool tree = connected && edges.size() == VERTS.size() - 1;\n    cout << name << \": \" << edges.size() << \" edges, connected=\" << (connected ? \"yes\" : \"no\") << \" -> \" << (tree ? \"tree\" : \"not a tree\") << \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector<Edge> tree = {{\"A\", \"B\"}, {\"A\", \"C\"}, {\"B\", \"D\"}, {\"B\", \"E\"}, {\"C\", \"F\"}};\n    vector<Edge> campus = {{\"A\", \"B\"}, {\"A\", \"C\"}, {\"B\", \"D\"}, {\"C\", \"D\"}, {\"D\", \"E\"}, {\"E\", \"F\"}};\n    check(\"T\", tree);\n    check(\"T without C-F\", vector<Edge>(tree.begin(), tree.end() - 1));\n    check(\"Campus map\", campus);\n\n    // Adding any edge to a tree closes exactly one cycle: the tree path between its ends plus the new edge\n    vector<string> ignored;\n    auto parent = bfsParents(tree, \"D\", ignored);\n    vector<string> path;\n    for (string x = \"F\"; !x.empty(); x = parent[x]) path.push_back(x);\n    reverse(path.begin(), path.end());\n    cout << \"Tree path D to F: \" << join(path, \" -> \") << \"\\n\";\n    path.push_back(\"D\");\n    cout << \"Adding D-F closes the cycle: \" << join(path, \" -> \") << \"\\n\";\n\n    // BFS keeps only the edge that discovered each vertex: a spanning tree of the campus map\n    vector<string> spanning;\n    bfsParents(campus, \"A\", spanning);\n    cout << \"BFS spanning tree of the campus map: \" << join(spanning, \", \") << \" (\" << spanning.size() << \" = \" << VERTS.size() << \" - 1 edges)\\n\";\n}\n",
            "javascript": "// A tree is connected with exactly n - 1 edges: one path between any two vertices, no cycles\nconst VERTS = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\"];\nconst TREE = [[\"A\", \"B\"], [\"A\", \"C\"], [\"B\", \"D\"], [\"B\", \"E\"], [\"C\", \"F\"]];\nconst CAMPUS = [[\"A\", \"B\"], [\"A\", \"C\"], [\"B\", \"D\"], [\"C\", \"D\"], [\"D\", \"E\"], [\"E\", \"F\"]];\n\nfunction bfsParents(verts, edges, start) {\n  const adj = Object.fromEntries(verts.map((v) => [v, []]));\n  for (const [u, v] of edges) {\n    adj[u].push(v);\n    adj[v].push(u);\n  }\n  const parent = { [start]: null }, queue = [start], found = [];\n  while (queue.length) {\n    const u = queue.shift();\n    for (const w of [...adj[u]].sort()) {\n      if (!(w in parent)) {\n        parent[w] = u;\n        found.push(u + \"-\" + w); // the edge that discovered w\n        queue.push(w);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return { parent, found };\n}\n\nfunction check(name, edges) {\n  const { parent } = bfsParents(VERTS, edges, \"A\");\n  const connected = Object.keys(parent).length === VERTS.length;\n  const tree = connected && edges.length === VERTS.length - 1;\n  console.log(name + \": \" + edges.length + \" edges, connected=\" + (connected ? \"yes\" : \"no\") + \" -> \" + (tree ? \"tree\" : \"not a tree\"));\n}\n\ncheck(\"T\", TREE);\ncheck(\"T without C-F\", TREE.slice(0, -1));\ncheck(\"Campus map\", CAMPUS);\n\n// Adding any edge to a tree closes exactly one cycle: the tree path between its ends plus the new edge\nconst { parent } = bfsParents(VERTS, TREE, \"D\");\nconst path = [];\nfor (let x = \"F\"; x !== null; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);\npath.reverse();\nconsole.log(\"Tree path D to F: \" + path.join(\" -> \"));\nconsole.log(\"Adding D-F closes the cycle: \" + [...path, \"D\"].join(\" -> \"));\n\n// BFS keeps only the edge that discovered each vertex: a spanning tree of the campus map\nconst { found } = bfsParents(VERTS, CAMPUS, \"A\");\nconsole.log(\"BFS spanning tree of the campus map: \" + found.join(\", \") + \" (\" + found.length + \" = \" + VERTS.length + \" - 1 edges)\");\n"
          },
          "output": {
            "python": "T: 5 edges, connected=yes -> tree\nT without C-F: 4 edges, connected=no -> not a tree\nCampus map: 6 edges, connected=yes -> not a tree\nTree path D to F: D -> B -> A -> C -> F\nAdding D-F closes the cycle: D -> B -> A -> C -> F -> D\nBFS spanning tree of the campus map: A-B, A-C, B-D, D-E, E-F (5 = 6 - 1 edges)",
            "java": "T: 5 edges, connected=yes -> tree\nT without C-F: 4 edges, connected=no -> not a tree\nCampus map: 6 edges, connected=yes -> not a tree\nTree path D to F: D -> B -> A -> C -> F\nAdding D-F closes the cycle: D -> B -> A -> C -> F -> D\nBFS spanning tree of the campus map: A-B, A-C, B-D, D-E, E-F (5 = 6 - 1 edges)",
            "javascript": "T: 5 edges, connected=yes -> tree\nT without C-F: 4 edges, connected=no -> not a tree\nCampus map: 6 edges, connected=yes -> not a tree\nTree path D to F: D -> B -> A -> C -> F\nAdding D-F closes the cycle: D -> B -> A -> C -> F -> D\nBFS spanning tree of the campus map: A-B, A-C, B-D, D-E, E-F (5 = 6 - 1 edges)",
            "cpp": "T: 5 edges, connected=yes -> tree\nT without C-F: 4 edges, connected=no -> not a tree\nCampus map: 6 edges, connected=yes -> not a tree\nTree path D to F: D -> B -> A -> C -> F\nAdding D-F closes the cycle: D -> B -> A -> C -> F -> D\nBFS spanning tree of the campus map: A-B, A-C, B-D, D-E, E-F (5 = 6 - 1 edges)"
          }
        }
