          "topic": "How the Internet Moves Data",
          "title": {
            "en": "Packets, addresses and DNS",
            "es": "Paquetes, direcciones y DNS"
          },
          "description": {
            "en": "Simulates the trip of a web request: looking up an IP address in a tiny DNS table, splitting an HTTP request into numbered packets, routing them hop by hop, losing and resending one, and reassembling them.",
            "es": "Simula el viaje de una petición web: buscar una dirección IP en una pequeña tabla DNS, dividir una petición HTTP en paquetes numerados, enrutarlos salto a salto, perder y reenviar uno, y volver a juntarlos."
          },
          "explanation": {
            "en": "When you open a website, a lot happens in a fraction of a second. This simulation walks through it with plain Python data instead of a real network.\n\nWhat to notice:\n- **DNS**: computers find each other by IP address (like `93.184.215.14`), not by name. The browser first asks DNS, the internet's phone book, to translate the name. An unknown name fails right there.\n- **HTTP**: the browser's request is plain text, `GET /index.html HTTP/1.1` plus headers, a format you can read.\n- **Packets**: the request is split into small numbered pieces, each labeled with the source and destination address. Real packets carry up to about 1500 bytes; these carry 12 characters to keep the output short.\n- **Routing**: each packet hops between routers toward the destination. Different packets can take different routes and arrive out of order.\n- **TCP**: sequence numbers let the receiver put packets back in order and notice missing ones. Packet 2 is \"lost\" and resent, and the message is reassembled exactly.\n\nReal HTTPS also encrypts all of this, which the Cybersecurity course covers.",
            "es": "Cuando abres un sitio web pasan muchas cosas en una fracción de segundo. Esta simulación lo recorre con datos simples de Python en lugar de una red real.\n\nQué observar:\n- **DNS**: las computadoras se encuentran por dirección IP (como `93.184.215.14`), no por nombre. El navegador primero le pide a DNS, el directorio de internet, que traduzca el nombre. Un nombre desconocido falla ahí mismo.\n- **HTTP**: la petición del navegador es texto simple, `GET /index.html HTTP/1.1` más encabezados, un formato que puedes leer.\n- **Paquetes**: la petición se divide en piezas pequeñas numeradas, cada una etiquetada con la dirección de origen y de destino. Los paquetes reales llevan hasta unos 1500 bytes; estos llevan 12 caracteres para que la salida sea corta.\n- **Enrutamiento**: cada paquete salta entre enrutadores hacia el destino. Distintos paquetes pueden tomar rutas distintas y llegar desordenados.\n- **TCP**: los números de secuencia permiten al receptor reordenar los paquetes y notar los que faltan. El paquete 2 se \"pierde\" y se reenvía, y el mensaje se reconstruye exacto.\n\nEl HTTPS real además cifra todo esto, que se ve en el curso de Ciberseguridad."
          },
          "code": {
            "python": "dns = {\"example.com\": \"93.184.215.14\", \"atlas.dev\": \"203.0.113.7\"}\nfor name in [\"example.com\", \"nothing.invalid\"]:\n    print(f\"DNS lookup {name}: {dns.get(name, 'not found')}\")\n\nmy_ip, server_ip = \"192.168.1.20\", dns[\"example.com\"]\nrequest = \"GET /index.html HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: example.com\\r\\n\\r\\n\"\nsize = 12\npackets = [{\"seq\": n, \"src\": my_ip, \"dst\": server_ip, \"data\": request[i:i + size]}\n           for n, i in enumerate(range(0, len(request), size))]\nprint(f\"\\nrequest split into {len(packets)} packets of up to {size} characters\")\n\nroute = [\"home router\", \"ISP\", \"backbone\", \"server's network\"]\nprint(\"route:\", \" -> \".join(route))\n\norder = [1, 3, 0]                  # packet 2 was lost, the rest arrived out of order\nreceived = {packets[n][\"seq\"]: packets[n][\"data\"] for n in order}\nmissing = [p[\"seq\"] for p in packets if p[\"seq\"] not in received]\nprint(\"arrived:\", order, \"| missing:\", missing)\nfor seq in missing:\n    received[seq] = packets[seq][\"data\"]\n    print(f\"resend packet {seq}\")\n\nmessage = \"\".join(received[seq] for seq in sorted(received))\nprint(\"reassembled first line:\", message.split(\"\\r\\n\")[0])\nprint(\"identical to what was sent:\", message == request)\n"
          },
          "output": {
            "python": "DNS lookup example.com: 93.184.215.14\nDNS lookup nothing.invalid: not found\n\nrequest split into 4 packets of up to 12 characters\nroute: home router -> ISP -> backbone -> server's network\narrived: [1, 3, 0] | missing: [2]\nresend packet 2\nreassembled first line: GET /index.html HTTP/1.1\nidentical to what was sent: True"
          }
