          "description": {
            "en": "Fills the 0/1 knapsack table bottom-up row by row and prints it, then compresses it to a single row by iterating the capacity downward, and shows the wrong answer an upward loop gives.",
            "es": "Llena la tabla de la mochila 0/1 de abajo hacia arriba, fila por fila, y la imprime; luego la comprime a una sola fila recorriendo la capacidad hacia abajo y muestra la respuesta incorrecta que da un ciclo hacia arriba."
          },
          "explanation": {
            "en": "Tabulation computes the same recurrence bottom-up: allocate a table indexed by the state, fill the base cases, and fill the remaining cells in an order where every cell's dependencies are already done. No recursion, no stack overflow, and loops that are easy to optimize.\n\nWalkthrough:\n- `dp[i][c]` is the best value using the first i items with capacity c. Row 0 (no items) is all zeros. Each cell is `max(dp[i-1][c], dp[i-1][c - w] + v)`, so it only reads the row above. Filling row by row, left to right, is a valid order.\n- The printed table lets you trace the answer: the bottom-right cell is the optimum (here 9), and comparing each row with the one above tells you which items were taken.\n- Space optimization: because row i only needs row i − 1, one array is enough, if the capacity loop runs downward. Going from high c to low c, `dp[c - w]` still holds the previous row's value when it is read.\n- Running the loop upward instead reads values already updated in the current row, which lets the same item be taken several times. That silently solves the unbounded knapsack instead, and the program shows the inflated answer.\n\nCost: O(n · W) time; O(n · W) space for the full table or O(W) for the single row (but then you can no longer reconstruct which items were chosen). Common mistake: getting the fill order wrong, so a cell reads a neighbour that has not been computed yet and silently uses 0.",
            "es": "La tabulación calcula la misma recurrencia de abajo hacia arriba: se reserva una tabla indexada por el estado, se llenan los casos base y se llenan las demás celdas en un orden en el que todas las dependencias de cada celda ya están calculadas. Sin recursión, sin desbordamiento de pila y con ciclos fáciles de optimizar.\n\nRecorrido:\n- `dp[i][c]` es el mejor valor usando los primeros i objetos con capacidad c. La fila 0 (sin objetos) es toda ceros. Cada celda es `max(dp[i-1][c], dp[i-1][c - w] + v)`, así que solo lee la fila de arriba. Llenar fila por fila, de izquierda a derecha, es un orden válido.\n- La tabla impresa permite seguir la respuesta: la celda inferior derecha es el óptimo (aquí 9), y comparar cada fila con la de arriba indica qué objetos se tomaron.\n- Optimización de espacio: como la fila i solo necesita la fila i − 1, basta un arreglo si el ciclo de capacidad va hacia abajo. Al ir de c alto a c bajo, `dp[c - w]` todavía guarda el valor de la fila anterior cuando se lee.\n- Si el ciclo va hacia arriba, lee valores ya actualizados en la fila actual, lo que permite tomar el mismo objeto varias veces. Eso resuelve sin avisar la mochila no acotada, y el programa muestra la respuesta inflada.\n\nCosto: O(n · W) en tiempo; O(n · W) en espacio para la tabla completa u O(W) para una sola fila (aunque entonces ya no se puede reconstruir qué objetos se eligieron). Error común: equivocarse en el orden de llenado, de modo que una celda lee una vecina aún no calculada y usa 0 sin avisar."
          },
          "code": {
            "java": "public class KnapsackTabulation {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] w = {1, 3, 4, 5};\n        int[] v = {2, 4, 5, 7};\n        int capacity = 7, n = w.length;\n\n        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][capacity + 1];            // row 0: no items, all zeros\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c <= capacity; c++) {\n                dp[i][c] = dp[i - 1][c];                       // skip\n                if (w[i - 1] <= c) dp[i][c] = Math.max(dp[i][c], dp[i - 1][c - w[i - 1]] + v[i - 1]);   // take\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"items (w,v): (1,2) (3,4) (4,5) (5,7), capacity \" + capacity);\n        StringBuilder header = new StringBuilder(\"        c:\");\n        for (int c = 0; c <= capacity; c++) header.append(String.format(\"%3d\", c));\n        System.out.println(header);\n        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n            StringBuilder row = new StringBuilder(String.format(\"%-10s\", i == 0 ? \"no items\" : \"item \" + i));\n            for (int c = 0; c <= capacity; c++) row.append(String.format(\"%3d\", dp[i][c]));\n            System.out.println(row);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"best = \" + dp[n][capacity]);\n\n        int[] down = new int[capacity + 1], up = new int[capacity + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int c = capacity; c >= w[i]; c--) down[c] = Math.max(down[c], down[c - w[i]] + v[i]);   // correct\n            for (int c = w[i]; c <= capacity; c++) up[c] = Math.max(up[c], up[c - w[i]] + v[i]);         // reuses items\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"one row, capacity loop downward: \" + down[capacity]);\n        System.out.println(\"one row, capacity loop upward:   \" + up[capacity] + \" (takes item (1,2) seven times)\");\n    }\n}\n",
            "python": "w = [1, 3, 4, 5]\nv = [2, 4, 5, 7]\ncapacity, n = 7, len(w)\n\ndp = [[0] * (capacity + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]  # row 0: no items, all zeros\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    for c in range(capacity + 1):\n        dp[i][c] = dp[i - 1][c]  # skip\n        if w[i - 1] <= c:\n            dp[i][c] = max(dp[i][c], dp[i - 1][c - w[i - 1]] + v[i - 1])  # take\nprint(f\"items (w,v): (1,2) (3,4) (4,5) (5,7), capacity {capacity}\")\nprint(\"        c:\" + \"\".join(f\"{c:3d}\" for c in range(capacity + 1)))\nfor i in range(n + 1):\n    label = \"no items\" if i == 0 else f\"item {i}\"\n    print(f\"{label:<10}\" + \"\".join(f\"{x:3d}\" for x in dp[i]))\nprint(\"best =\", dp[n][capacity])\n\ndown, up = [0] * (capacity + 1), [0] * (capacity + 1)\nfor i in range(n):\n    for c in range(capacity, w[i] - 1, -1):\n        down[c] = max(down[c], down[c - w[i]] + v[i])  # correct\n    for c in range(w[i], capacity + 1):\n        up[c] = max(up[c], up[c - w[i]] + v[i])        # reuses items\nprint(\"one row, capacity loop downward:\", down[capacity])\nprint(\"one row, capacity loop upward:  \", up[capacity], \"(takes item (1,2) seven times)\")\n"
          },
          "output": {
            "java": "items (w,v): (1,2) (3,4) (4,5) (5,7), capacity 7\n        c:  0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7\nno items    0  0  0  0  0  0  0  0\nitem 1      0  2  2  2  2  2  2  2\nitem 2      0  2  2  4  6  6  6  6\nitem 3      0  2  2  4  6  7  7  9\nitem 4      0  2  2  4  6  7  9  9\nbest = 9\none row, capacity loop downward: 9\none row, capacity loop upward:   14 (takes item (1,2) seven times)",
            "python": "items (w,v): (1,2) (3,4) (4,5) (5,7), capacity 7\n        c:  0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7\nno items    0  0  0  0  0  0  0  0\nitem 1      0  2  2  2  2  2  2  2\nitem 2      0  2  2  4  6  6  6  6\nitem 3      0  2  2  4  6  7  7  9\nitem 4      0  2  2  4  6  7  9  9\nbest = 9\none row, capacity loop downward: 9\none row, capacity loop upward:   14 (takes item (1,2) seven times)"
          }
